@@ -333,7 +333,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **Feature-removal health handling in `CvUnitAI::AI_bestCityBuild`:** forests provide city health, so non-bonus forest chops are **weighted by city population and health surplus**; **small cities** can still chop, but when health is tight the AI usually **prefers other useful improvements** because removing forest health can make the city unhealthy or more unhealthy. **Jungle is different** because it **adds unhealth** and is **generally targeted** for removal/improvement. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5941+) For **medium/large cities**, non-bonus forested BFC plots have a **softer health penalty**. This is because later in the game we usually have many health sources (trade, owned bonuses, buildings, techs, etc.), **higher population** can use or needs more improved BFC plots, and **improved yields are stronger**, so leaving useful forested tiles unimproved forever can be worse than accepting the forest-health loss.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6018+) **Compare the best useful work across all cities:** workers now compare each city's strongest reachable job by its complete Food/Production/Commerce/resource/terrain evaluation, with only a modest travel cost, instead of favoring cities mainly by population or staying near weaker local work. Cities that lack enough completed or already-assigned improvements for their population receive proportionally more help; counting assigned workers discourages crowding one city, while reserving one worker per plot prevents several workers from targeting the same tile. In BBAI testing in save file 442, this redirected workers from low-value unused Tundra Workshops toward valuable Grass/Plains Hill Mines in underdeveloped cities. See also [KI#39](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#39---seemingly-fixed-orand-enhanced-make-ai-workers-move-sooner-to-city-b-or-city-c-or-such-and-vice-versa-if-current-city-is-already-improved-enough-and-dont-need-to-be-over-improved-while-other-cities-are-not-improved-enough-and-would-much-rather-need-it) and [KI#41](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#41---seemingly-fixed-beyond-tremendously-improved-ai-worker-mobility-flexibility-and-reliability-now-favouring-minimal-big-city-improvement-come-back-to-it-later-but-dont-delay-improving-smaller-ones-quick-moving-to-smaller-ones-and-spending-longer-to-improve-smaller-ones-as-they-grow-fast-but-as-well-as-being-braver-in-our-own-cultural-borders-orand-moving-to-other-cities-needing-improvements-rather-than-being-parked-in-current-city-if-im-not-mistaken-but-and-such-other-changes-to-increase-ai-efficiency-reliably-and-other-changes-if-any-thanks-to-chatgpt-5-and-me-too-if-i-may-say-but).
 - **Worker mobility / overimprovement refinement:** workers still rotate away from sufficiently improved small cities, but medium and large cities are less likely to leave useful BFC plots unimproved. Workers also avoid ordinary replacement churn (e.g. **Mine ↔ Windmill**, **Cottage ↔ Farm**) while valid unimproved BFC work remains, except for bonus fixes and irrigation-chain Farms. The small-city rotation shortcut does not make a worker leave when it is already standing on this same city's (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6018+) useful BFC plot and that plot is the city's best current job.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6018+) **Finish valuable followup builds after feature removal:** a worker can complete standalone Forest/Jungle/Fallout removal (including receiving Forest chop production sooner, separated from e.g. a 2 in 1 mine that would deliver hammer on feature remove later plus would be less flexible), then continue directly into the worthwhile Mine, Farm, Cottage, resource improvement, etc. selected for that plot. This prevents workers from chopping or clearing a strong tile, walking away, and paying another trip later (which is especially inefficient if this costs movement/delay again); weak ordinary follow-ups are still reconsidered instead of being forced. Queued follow-ups now wait for the worker's next turn when removal consumes all movement rather than being silently discarded.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6018+) **Finish valuable followup builds after feature removal:** a worker can complete standalone Forest/Jungle/Fallout removal (including receiving Forest chop production sooner, separated from e.g. a 2 in 1 mine that would deliver hammer on feature remove later plus would be less flexible), then continue directly into the worthwhile Mine, Farm, Cottage, resource improvement, etc. selected for that plot. This prevents workers from chopping or clearing a strong tile, walking away, and paying another trip later (which is especially inefficient if this costs movement/delay again); weak ordinary follow-ups are still reconsidered instead of being forced. Queued follow-ups now wait for the worker's next turn when removal consumes all movement rather than being silently discarded. See also [KI#176](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#176---fixed-base-advcivk-mod-mission-queue-bug-exposed-by-advciv-sas-worker-follow-up-improvements-stale-ai-worker-build-target-could-execute-on-the-wrong-plot).
 - **Retreat logic:** fix excessive retreat → "parking in city" edge cases with a wake-from-retreat and saner thresholds. See [KI#50](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#50---tremendously-improvedfixedenhanced-excessive-ai-worker-retreat-logic-causing-worker-parking-in-cities-in-rare-cases-now-added-a-wake-from-retreat-and-other-changes-if-any-other-change-i-mean).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5918+) **Worker irrigation-chain hunting:** updated SAS AI worker logic so it now **starts from actual dry-farm / low-food BFC targets** and **hunts short owned irrigation-chain steps**, including first links **outside the city BFC**. This fixed Chaco Canyon and Harappan-style cases where a nearby (2-3 plots away from BFC) water source existed but BFC-only worker logic missed the first link. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6018+) Workers now retain only the strongest available connector toward each target instead of treating several alternative routes to the same Farm as separate urgent jobs, leaving other workers free to pursue valuable improvements elsewhere.

